146:      "criterion": "to_domain_error constructs DomainError::new(code, format!("{}: {head}", self.monokl_code())) where head is the first line of self's own thiserror-generated Display text (self.to_string(), respecting whichever #[error(...)] format each of the 14 variants locks per SPEC-001 AC-001 -- including Io's #[error(transparent)], where this is identical to the wrapped FileIoError's own Display, per thiserror's documented transparent behavior). Every line after head comes from a separate chain-flattening walk that starts at self.source() -- not at self, which head already covers -- and continues through that error's own .source(), and so on until None; this ordering matters and is not interchangeable with starting the walk at self.source() directly (skipping self's own message): for Walk { path, #[source] source: ignore::Error }, self.source() yields only the wrapped ignore::Error's own message, never the path field, which exists solely in Walk's own Display text; for Json(#[from] serde_json::Error) (not #[error(transparent)], unlike Io), self's own Display carries monokl-specific framing around the wrapped serde_json::Error's message that self.source() alone would not reproduce -- so head must always be self's own Display, never self.source()'s, or these two variants silently lose real information, contradicting this track's own information-preserving purpose. Both head and every line the source-walk produces are joined by first splitting each link's own Display output (self's own Display for head, and every source link's own Display for the walk) on any of the three line-boundary forms -- '\r\n' (checked first, consuming both characters as one boundary, never leaving a dangling '\n' to also match the standalone-'\n' rule), standalone '\n', or standalone '\r' (a bare carriage return with no following '\n', which some subprocess output uses for in-place progress updates) -- before treating each resulting line as a separate entry in the walk -- this is necessary because a single link in the chain (e.g. MonoklError::Git's message field -- built from gix's own error Display text, not a git subprocess's stderr: monokl uses gix, a native Rust git implementation, and git_scope.rs's own construction sites format! this field directly from a gix error value, e.g. format!("{base} {head}: {err}") and format!("{git_ref}:{path}: {err}") at 03-multi-language-platform.md:1395-1397 and :1540-1551 -- neither of which shells out to a git binary) can, defensively, itself contain embedded newlines -- no cited construction site is confirmed to actually produce one, but nothing in gix's own Display contract rules it out either, so this module guards against the possibility rather than assuming it away, and DomainError::render() (verified against michi's real source, crates/michi-core/src/error.rs and hints.rs, at the pinned rev per AC-015) writes message as a single raw `message: {text}
` line and writes hints, separately, via append_hints() as a COUNTED block -- `help[{hints.len()}]:
` followed by exactly hints.len() lines, each `  {hint}
` (two-space indented) -- with no newline sanitization applied to either. An un-flattened multi-line message therefore emits extra physical lines carrying no `message:`/`help[`/`  ` prefix at all, indistinguishable from whatever block follows; an un-flattened multi-line hint desynchronizes the count render() already declared in `help[N]:` from the number of physical lines that actually follow it, since N is fixed at hints.len() (the number of Hint values passed in) before any individual hint's own embedded newlines are considered -- a line-oriented parser reading exactly N declared hint lines would read past the intended block into that hint's own later lines, or (for the last hint) into whatever comes after the hints block entirely. Every line after the first (across the whole flattened walk, not just the first link) becomes a separate .hint(line) call on the DomainError builder, in the order the walk produced them. If self has no source() at all (Self::source() returns None, e.g. for TooManyTerms or TokenizerInit, whose thiserror #[error(...)] attributes carry no #[source] field per SPEC-001 AC-001's own field-shape lock), the source-walk that would otherwise start at self.source() produces zero further lines, so no hints are added by this step beyond head itself (though monokl_code()-specific enrichment per AC-012, not this criterion, may still add hints for TooManyTerms specifically).",

