      "id": "AC-001",
      "criterion": "DependencyRecord (01-core-architecture.md:314-319) compiles with exactly 3 fields in this order: line: usize, bindings: Vec<DependencyBinding>, target: DependencyTarget. It carries exactly two attribute lines (#[derive(Debug, Clone, Serialize, Deserialize)], #[serde(rename_all = \"camelCase\")]) -- no Copy/PartialEq/Eq. Only bindings carries a field-level attribute: #[serde(default)] with no skip_serializing_if (line 316-317); line and target carry no field-level attributes and are required -- a JSON object omitting either \"line\" or \"target\" fails to deserialize with a missing-field error naming that field, the same consequence DependencyBinding's three required fields produce (AC-002). Consequence for bindings: a DependencyRecord whose bindings vec is empty still serializes the key (\"bindings\":[]), unlike a skip_serializing_if field which would omit it; on deserialize, an object omitting the \"bindings\" key entirely succeeds, defaulting to an empty vec via that explicit #[serde(default)] -- and, unlike an Option-typed field (where #[serde(default)] is redundant with the type's own implicit missing-key leniency, per SPEC-003 AC-005), this attribute is NOT redundant here: Vec<T> receives no implicit leniency from serde's derive macro, so without #[serde(default)] a missing \"bindings\" key would fail with a missing-field error instead. Contrast with JsxElementEntry.attributes (AC-011), a Vec field with no such attribute.",
