  "linked_requirement": "REQ-005",
  "linked_research": "docs/spec/01-core-architecture.md:312-452 (Section 4 types.rs verbatim -- the primary source for every type and method in this track; the \"Post-research correction\" note at lines 397-400); docs/spec/03-multi-language-platform.md:2405-2417 (independent restatement of RustPathAnchor), :2419-2442 (DependencyTarget), :2381-2403 (LangData plus its two methods) -- all confirmed identical to the canonical source; :1889 (a DependencyTarget::Namespace pattern-match arm), :1979 and :1155 (Namespace-consuming, not constructing, helper code), all cited for AC-007; docs/spec/07-edge-cases-and-failure-modes.md (Part 3 finding #20, DependencyTarget::Namespace as C#-roadmap residue); docs/spec/05-research-and-decisions.md \u00a76 (the C#-dropped-from-the-Go/Java-tier decision) and \u00a710 (the generic tree-sitter fallback tier's launch set, which reintroduces C#, cited for AC-007's corrected scoping); .claude/specs/SPEC-003.json (AC-002, AC-005, AC-010's established serde-semantics findings -- variant-level vs. field-level camelCase transform rules, Option<T>'s implicit missing-key deserialize leniency -- re-applied and, where the underlying mechanism differs, explicitly contrasted rather than assumed to generalize, e.g. AC-001/AC-011's Vec<T>-does-not-share-that-leniency finding).",
  "revision_note": "Fifth draft -- correction pass (canon-drafter, correction mode), triggered by a contradiction vector-challenger found during PLAN-005's plan review and confirmed empirically against a compiled fixture matching this spec's own AC-003/AC-004 (BindingKind) and AC-008 (RustPathAnchor): AC-019 as previously written was FALSE for RustPathAnchor and VACUOUS (neither true nor false) for BindingKind. (1) RustPathAnchor: because RustPathAnchor carries no container-level tag/content attribute, serde uses its default externally-tagged representation -- three variants as bare strings, Extern(String) as a single-key object. External tagging requires the JSON to be exactly a single-key map naming one recognized variant, so a second, unrecognized sibling key does not get silently discarded the way AC-019 claimed for the rest of the cluster -- it breaks the required shape and deserialization fails outright. Empirically confirmed: serde_json::from_str::<RustPathAnchor>(r#\"{\\\"extern\\\":\\\"foo\\\",\\\"bogusKey\\\":123}\"#) errors ('expected value at line 1 column 15'), it does not silently succeed. (2) BindingKind: a fieldless enum whose JSON representation is always a bare string (e.g. \"named\"), never a JSON object at all -- AC-019's premise ('a JSON object with an extra, unrecognized key') cannot apply to it, so listing it among the 10 types AC-019 covered was a category error, not merely an inaccurate detail. The other 12 of the original 14 named types remain correctly described by AC-019's silent-discard claim, empirically re-confirmed for one representative of each of the 4 distinct serde-shape categories among them (ExportRecord: plain struct; RustData: empty struct; DependencyTarget: internally-tagged enum; LangData: adjacently-tagged enum).\n\nFixed by narrowing AC-019 to exactly these 12 types, with an explicit clause stating why BindingKind and RustPathAnchor are excluded rather than silently dropped from its list. Added AC-019B, stating RustPathAnchor's actual behavior (a second key alongside its single externally-tagged key is rejected, not discarded), so this cluster's serde contract carries no silent gap where AC-019 used to overclaim. Added AC-004B, stating BindingKind's actual unknown-value behavior (an unrecognized string fails with a serde unknown-variant error -- the same outcome SPEC-003 AC-009 established for SymbolKind and Visibility, two fieldless enums BindingKind's own derive list was built to match per AC-003; a non-string JSON value fails instead with a type-mismatch error) -- chosen over leaving the exclusion as a bare, unexplained carve-out, matching this project's standing practice (established across SPEC-001 and this spec's own earlier revision rounds) of stating every observable serde consequence explicitly rather than leaving it to be inferred from a cited analogy, even a strong one. Both new criteria's IDs follow this project's established <base-criterion>B suffix convention for a criterion tightly coupled to, and placed immediately after, an existing one -- confirmed against SPEC-001's own usage (AC-002B follows AC-002, AC-013B follows AC-013, AC-020B follows AC-020, read fresh from SPEC-001.json for this correction). Note: the specific precedent named when this correction was requested, 'SPEC-001's AC-004B,' does not exist in SPEC-001 as read from disk -- SPEC-001 has a task named T-004B (a plan-level docs-correction task, not a spec criterion), and the acceptance-criterion suffix convention actually comes from AC-002B/AC-013B/AC-020B instead. The pattern itself is real and was followed correctly; only that one example ID was misremembered.\n\nDocs-correction check (per AGENTS.md's spec-divergence rule, the same rule SPEC-001's T-004B applied): re-read docs/spec/01-core-architecture.md:312-452 in full for this correction -- it contains no prose claim anywhere in that range about unrecognized-key handling, deny_unknown_fields, or unknown-variant rejection for any type in this cluster. AC-019's original 'silently discarded' claim was this spec's own inference from the absence of #[serde(deny_unknown_fields)] in the verbatim derive lists, not a restatement of something the source prose independently asserted. Also checked docs/spec/03-multi-language-platform.md (which independently restates RustPathAnchor and LangData) and docs/spec/05-research-and-decisions.md -- neither makes this claim either. There is therefore nothing in the docs corpus to correct, and no docs/spec/05-research-and-decisions.md entry is needed for this correction -- unlike T-004B's case, which corrected a prose Display-literal string the source document actually contained, this correction fixes an inference this spec added on its own, with no corresponding prose to diverge from.\n\nNo criteria beyond AC-019 (narrowed) and the two new additions (AC-004B, AC-019B) were touched. AC-014's existing reference to AC-019 ('silently discarded per AC-019') concerns only the LangData payload's \"data\" object -- TsData or one of the four empty per-language data structs -- all of which remain inside AC-019's narrowed 12-type scope, so AC-014 required no change.\n\nFourth draft, after canon-exit-gate's second pass (retry 2, fail on one narrow finding out of 19 criteria, 18 confirmed clean via live compiled-toolchain re-derivation): AC-007's consumed-not-constructed clause attributed the line-1979 quote to dependency_target_matches, but that quote's actual source is a different, similarly-named helper, dependency_target_resolved -- dependency_target_matches is the function at line 1155, described differently there. Fixed to attribute each quote to its correct helper.\n\nThird draft, after canon-exit-gate's first pass (retry 1, fail). The gate found the single most consequential defect in this track: AC-005 claimed DependencyTarget's struct-variant fields (is_relative, is_static) get camelCase-renamed by the enum's container-level #[serde(rename_all = \"camelCase\")] -- false. That attribute