w round. canon-verifier found 17 of 18 criteria and both api_surface methods cleanly supported, catching one real overclaim: AC-015 attributed GoData/JavaData's future field-growth compatibility to LangData's own #[non_exhaustive] attribute, conflating an enum-level exhaustive-match protection with a struct-level field-growth concern the four per-language data structs' own (non-#[non_exhaustive]) declarations don't actually have -- fixed to state the two as separate, unresolved questions. canon-auditor found: AC-007's negative claim ('no analyzer... planned... constructs Namespace') didn't reconcile with 05-research-and-decisions.md \u00a710, which reintroduces C# (the variant's original motivating language) as a decided fallback-tier launch-set target in the same document -- fixed to scope the claim to the current verbatim code only, not to planned-but-unbuilt work, with a corpus-wide-grep citation added matching this project's established practice for negative claims; the corresponding non_goal updated to match. Uneven is_error_case coverage across the required-field cluster: AC-001 stated no missing-field consequence for DependencyRecord's line/target (added, tagged true); AC-002 stated the consequence but was mistagged false (retagged true); AC-009/AC-010 stated only the label 'required' without the observable consequence (both extended and retagged true). AC-016's 'intentional, not an omission' clause asserted a fact about this document's own authorial intent rather than LangData's observable behavior -- replaced with a verifiable panic-freedom argument. A non_goals internal inconsistency ('outside the two locations this spec cites' with only one location actually named) fixed by citing the confirmed 03-multi-language-platform.md restatement of DependencyTarget/RustPathAnchor the auditor found and verified identical -- folded into scope and linked_research as a real cross-check per SPEC-003's established precedent, rather than left as an unchecked disclaimer.\n\nFirst draft. Drafted directly against primary source (01-core-architecture.md:312-452) with SPEC-001/SPEC-003/SPEC-004's established lessons applied proactively rather than discovered via a failed gate round: independently re-derived each serde-attribute's actual behavioral consequence rather than assuming a pattern found once (Option<T>'s implicit deserialize leniency, SPEC-003 AC-005) generalizes to a structurally different type (Vec<T> does not share it, confirmed via serde's own documented derive-macro special-casing, which is specific to Option-typed fields) rather than asserting it uniformly; distinguished #[non_exhaustive] (a Rust match-exhaustiveness attribute) from any effect on serde-generated (de)serialize code (it has none) at both of this cluster's #[non_exhaustive] enums (DependencyTarget, LangData) rather than stating it once and assuming the reader generalizes; and worked out, rather than assumed, which of RustPathAnchor's and LangData's explicit #[serde(rename = ...)] overrides are functionally necessary (change the JSON value from what the default transform would produce) versus redundant (restate what the default transform already produces) by computing the default transform for each variant name by hand before comparing it to the explicit rename.",
  "reasoning": "This track's central technical risk, identified before drafting rather than discovered via correction: two different serde enum-tagging strategies (DependencyTarget's internal tag=\"kind\" with no content key, LangData's adjacent tag=\"language\"+content=\"data\") coexist in the same small cluster, and it would be easy to describe one in terms that accidentally also describe the other, or to state a JSON shape for one that's actually the other's shape. Verified each independently against the verbatim source (lines 342 and 402 respectively) and stated their structural difference explicitly in both AC-005 and AC-012 rather than relying on the reader to infer it from two separately-read code blocks. Also treated Vec<T> and Option<T>'s field-attribute behavior as genuinely distinct questions rather than assuming SPEC-003's Option<T> finding transfers -- this is exactly the kind of unwarranted generalization that produced real, gate-caught defects in SPEC-003's earlier rounds (the false claim that a missing 'signature' key fails to deserialize), so this draft computed Vec<T>'s actual behavior (no implicit leniency; #[serde(default)] is load-bearing, not redundant) from first principles rather than assuming symmetry with the already-verified Option<T> case. Did not independently re-run serde/serde_json against a live toolchain for this draft (matching this project's now-established practice of flagging when a claim rests on documented library semantics rather than fresh empirical reproduction) -- the Vec<T>-vs-Option<T> distinction, the internal-vs-adjacent tagging JSON shapes, and the necessary-vs-redundant rename analysis all rest on serde's publicly documented, stable derive-macro behavior, but a review pass with toolchain access should still verify AC-001, AC-006, AC-011, and AC-014's specific claimed error messages/shapes empirically, consistent with the error class that has recurred across every behavioral track in this project so far."
}

